The way I see it is captured in the guide [here](https://docs.dronecore.io/v/develop/en/guide/taking_off_landing.html#health-check). 
```
// Wait until health is OK and vehicle is ready to arm
while (telemetry->health_all_ok() != true) {
    std::cout << "Vehicle not ready to arm ..." << std::endl;
    std::this_thread::sleep_for(std::chrono::seconds(1));
}
```
So basically you want to wait on readiness for arming, *and* you want to be able to dig into why you can't arm. The test there is `telemetry->health_all_ok()`. If that does not work, then either it needs to be:
- extended so it won't return OK until all required information has been retrieved OR
- we need `is_armable()` to do the same job.
I tend to think that `health_all_ok()` should not return until all information that is needed to assess that health is retrieved - ie landing state might be part of that info. If this cannot be in "health all OK" then I tend to think that API should be replaced by `is_armable` - it isn't useful if it can't be used for arm checking.

The other way to do this is of course to just try to arm and repeat until you get success. I don't like that as much because it is extra messages that need to be sent.

