Hi Jonas,

This looks like the commit that commented out the line:
system.second->system_impl()->process_mavlink_message(message);
and replaced it with the line:
mavlink_message_handler.process_message(message);

The line being replaced in this commit seems to route messages only to systems that match the sysid of the message. The new line does not look like it does this check, and when I look at the code in the MavlinkMessageHandler class it doesn't look like it distinguishes between callback registrations based on originating system and the sysid doesn't get used for deciding which callbacks to invoke for a given message. In other words, before this commit it looks like the intent was to route messages only to systems with matching sysid. After this commit it looks like all messages are sent to all systems, assuming they match the sysid of at least one existing system. I have not tried reverting to before this commit though since it is from about 1.5 years ago.

I see, you're talking about [this](https://github.com/mavlink/MAVSDK/blob/main/src/mavsdk/core/mavsdk_impl.cpp#L394-L400):

```cpp
    for (auto& system : _systems) {
        if (system.first == message.sysid) {
            // system.second->system_impl()->process_mavlink_message(message);
            mavlink_message_handler.process_message(message);
            break;
        }
    }
```

So before, the `process_message` was done at the system level, and the `if (system.first == message.sysid) {` would ensure that the system would process the message only if `message.sysid` was corresponding. Now it is not the case anymore, it will always call `mavlink_message_handler.process_message(message);` for the messages.

I only quickly looked there, but maybe [this function](https://github.com/mavlink/MAVSDK/blob/main/src/mavsdk/core/mavlink_message_handler.cpp#L55) should somehow check the system id? Maybe check that the entry corresponds to the system from which the message is coming from? Which means that maybe an `Entry` now needs the `system_id`, on top of the `component_id`.

