We don't have a standard layout for applications yet. If you have a look at any Android application for eg, it has a layout something like, 

```java
public class BasicClickHandlersActivity extends Activity {

@Override
protected void onCreate(Bundle savedInstanceState) {
super.onCreate(savedInstanceState);
setContentView(R.layout.activity_basic_click_handlers);
Button secondButton = (Button) findViewById(R.id.btnClick2);
secondButton.setOnClickListener(new View.OnClickListener() {
@Override
public void onClick(View v) {
secondButtonClicked(v);
}
});
}

@Override
public boolean onCreateOptionsMenu(Menu menu) {
// Inflate the menu; this adds items to the action bar if it is present.
getMenuInflater().inflate(R.menu.activity_basic_click_handlers, menu);
return true;
}
}
```

Its more clear for reader to visualize application's activity. Its clear that `onCreate()` is the entry point and application's business logic goes into one these callbacks. 

All DroneCore applications primarily deal with discovered device.

**Note:** I use term device until we merge code containing `System` terminology.

So, application won't do anything without device discovery. If there's no device discovered for 3 seconds, DroneCore fires timeout callback. So, its convenient if we layout a DroneCore application template similar to a below one:

![dronecore!UNITO-UNDERSCORE!app!UNITO-UNDERSCORE!layout](https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/26615772/37448299-ca079d6a-284b-11e8-9017-c0fb349039cb.png)

And, names `on_discover()`, `on_timeout()` seems more natural than `register_on_discover()`, etc.
Kindly share your thoughts on it.
Thanks.

